  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to employ Bayesian optimization with a Gaussian process (GP) surrogate model to efficiently explore the high-dimensional optimization space of step functions. This approach differs fundamentally from previous gradient-based or metaheuristic methods by adaptively focusing the search on promising regions of the feasible space while explicitly modeling the objective's structure. The key innovation is the use of a probabilistic model that captures the non-convex, noisy nature of the overlap integral objective and dynamically adjusts sampling based on uncertainty estimates. This addresses the local optimum bottleneck by prioritizing exploration of uncharted regions alongside exploitation of current best candidates.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: A piecewise-constant function h(x) with n_points samples over [0, 2], where 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx] * dx. Constraints:\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2 (equality)\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 (bound constraints)\n\nObjective function f(h) = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx). The value to minimize is computed exactly via np.correlate with full mode, matching the evaluator's definition. The GP surrogate models the function f(h), with inputs being the vector of h values and outputs being the predicted f(h), with uncertainty quantified via predictive variance.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Input: n_points = 400, initial_h_values (if available)\nb) Initialize GP: \n   - If initial_h_values exists:\n      i) Reshape to (n_points,)\n      ii) Normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\n      iii) Use as initial sample\n   - Else:\n      i) Generate 20 random initial points with h[i] ~ Uniform(0,1), then normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\nc) GP loop:\n   i) For iteration 1 to 500:\n      - Compute predictive mean and variance at current best h\n      - Select next candidate via Expected Improvement (EI) acquisition function\n      - Evaluate f(h) using exact evaluator\n      - Update GP with new sample (h, f(h))\nd) Candidate acceptance:\n   - Maintain a set of top 5 candidates by f(h)\n   - Re-evaluate all candidates to ensure feasibility\ne) Final refinement:\n   - Use L-BFGS-B on top candidate to refine\n   - Enforce constraints via rescaling after clipping\nf) Output: best_h.tolist(), best_c5, n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400\n- Initial samples = 20\n- GP kernel = RBF(1.0) + WhiteNoise(1e-5)\n- Acquisition function = Expected Improvement\n- Iterations = 500\n- Refinement steps = 50\n- Tolerance = 1e-6\n\nVariations:\n- Kernel: Add Matern or Periodic kernel for non-smooth objectives\n- Acquisition function: Thompson Sampling or Upper Confidence Bound\n- Initial samples: Use parent if available\n- GP parameters: Learn kernel parameters from initial samples\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 100s: Initialization and GP training\n- 300s: Bayesian optimization loop\n- 100s: Final refinement and re-evaluation\n- 100s: Fallback and termination checks\n\nTermination checks:\n- After 500 iterations\n- If EI improvement < 1e-5 for 30 iterations\n- If remaining time < 30s\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Clip values to [0,1] after all operations\n- Use scipy.optimize.minimize with method='L-BFGS-B' for final refinement\n- Add penalty term \u03bb*(\u2211h - n/2)^2 to objective when necessary\n- Re-scale solutions after clipping using: h_scaled = h / \u2211h * (n/2)\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- np.allclose(np.sum(h), n_pts/2, atol=1e-6)\n- np.all((h >= 0) & (h <= 1))\n\nObjective consistency:\n- Re-evaluate final candidate using exact evaluator:\n  score = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nSanity checks:\n- score \u2264 0.38085\n- np.sum(h) == n_pts / 2\n- np.min(h) \u2265 0, np.max(h) \u2264 1\n\nFallback protocol:\n- If no valid solution found, return parent with penalty:\n  h_fallback = np.clip(h + 0.01 * np.random.randn(n_points), 0, 1)\n  score_fallback = max(np.correlate(h_fallback, 1-h_fallback, mode=\"full\") * dx) + 0.01",
